"""Original displacement and livelihood-support teaching; fictional cases."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-046","Climate Displacement and Livelihood Adaptation","climate-displacement-and-livelihood-adaptation","Climate Displacement\nand Livelihood\nAdaptation",
        "Prepare a bounded displacement and livelihood-support plan that respects changing preferences, checks service capacity and reviews rights and access without determining legal status.",[
m("Moving staying and changing choices","Distinguish a stated preference from a constraint without assigning a fixed mobility identity.",[
"Environmental change can interact with livelihoods, housing, relationships, services and public decisions to shape movement or staying. A flood preceding a move does not establish the event's attribution to climate change, nor does it establish that climate was the person's only reason. Describe the reported circumstances and the information source separately from an explanation still being investigated. The IPCC synthesis provides broad risk context; it cannot decide an individual's motives. In an introductory support conversation, ask what changed, what matters now and what the person wants help considering. [S2, R4]",
"Staying may reflect a valued attachment, a practical preference, constrained options or a combination that changes over time. Sengupta's review examines these relationships across uneven empirical contexts, including substantial Bangladesh evidence. It does not supply a validated Nigerian screening instrument or justify labelling someone permanently trapped. Ask about preferred options and the means of pursuing them separately. A person may want to stay but lack safe services, or want to move but lack accessible transport. Record the person's own account with appropriate privacy rather than infer willingness from current location. [R4, S1]",
"Different members of a household may have different priorities and opportunities to speak. Caring responsibilities, age, disability, work and access to documents can affect what is feasible, but they should not be assumed from a group label. Arrange a safe and accessible opportunity to contribute through authorised practice arrangements. Do not treat a household representative's answer as every member's consent. The local IJSDS study offers contextual questions about interacting livelihood and service constraints; its cross-sectional design does not predict this household's decisions or show what caused a particular outcome. [R8, S1]",
"Use mobility terms carefully. Displacement, voluntary movement and planned relocation raise different questions about agency, assistance and decision authority. This course does not assign refugee status, decide an entitlement or present a climate-refugee label as a legal determination. Record uncertainty and request appropriate protection or legal advice when needed. Summarise the present preference, constraints, immediate concern, agreed next step and review date. A changed preference is new information to consider, not evidence that the earlier account was false. Support should not depend on agreeing to an option the practitioner prefers. [S7, S1, R4]"],
"Fictional case: after repeated flooding, one resident wishes to remain near a trading network, while another wants to explore moving but lacks accessible transport. The practitioner records each account separately and checks authorised support options without deciding that either choice is freely available or permanently fixed.",
"Write two separate preference-and-constraint notes for the fictional residents. Identify one missing fact, one question that allows a changed preference and one referral question requiring appropriate authority.",
"A useful note separates what the resident prefers, what prevents an option and what remains uncertain. Current location alone cannot establish free choice, legal status or a permanent mobility category.",["S2","R4","S1","R8","S7"],[
("Environmental and social influences can interact.","A resident moved after flooding and loss of work. What can the support note establish from this account alone?","The reported sequence and concerns, with causal attribution still uncertain.",["That climate change was the sole cause of the move.","That the resident qualifies for a specific legal status.","That all residents affected by the flood wanted to move."],"A reported sequence is not individual causal attribution or a legal determination."),
("Preference and ability to act require separate questions.","A resident wants to explore moving but has no accessible transport. What distinction should be recorded?","The wish to consider movement and the practical constraint on pursuing it.",["A conclusion that remaining proves satisfaction.","A permanent label based solely on present location.","An assumption that accessible transport is already available."],"A preference does not establish the means of realising it."),
("Immobility is not a permanent identity.","A person changes their preferred option after service access improves. How should the practitioner respond?","Revisit the plan using the person's current account and circumstances.",["Reject the change because a previous category must remain fixed.","Conclude that every earlier statement was false.","Remove the person's opportunity to choose."],"Aspirations and constraints can change and should be reviewed."),
("Household members may hold different priorities.","Only a household representative attended a relocation discussion. What remains to check?","Whether other affected members have an appropriate opportunity to express their priorities.",["That attendance provides every member's consent.","That all members have identical livelihood needs.","That no accessible participation arrangement is necessary."],"One account need not represent every affected person."),
("Support conversations do not determine legal status.","A learner is asked to certify a resident as a climate refugee. What fits this course's role?","Document the concern and seek appropriate protection or legal advice.",["Issue a status decision from the course certificate.","Use a general climate report as a legal ruling.","Guarantee eligibility for any destination's assistance."],"Legal questions require the relevant authority and context."),
]),
m("Livelihood continuity and support pathways","Build a feasible support pathway around a person's priorities and verified receiving capacity.",[
"Livelihood adaptation concerns the conditions that allow people to sustain a living, not simply a list of alternative jobs. Ask about earning activities, unpaid care, accessible services, transport, tools, documents and relationships that matter to the person's preferred option. Discuss what can continue, what has been disrupted and which change the person wants to explore. Avoid asking for unnecessary details about income or identity when a less intrusive question would answer the support need. Climate and contextual social research help frame these questions, while an actual plan requires direct discussion and appropriate local evidence. [S2, R8, S1]",
"A referral is a possible pathway until the receiving service confirms its function, capacity, eligibility process and accessible contact arrangements. Check who can make the next contact and what information is genuinely required, within the relevant consent and lawful information-sharing process. Do not promise funding, employment, admission or replacement documents on another organisation's behalf. Explain any unresolved condition and agree a feasible follow-up. The displacement guidance supports coordinated access to rights and livelihoods, but a name in a directory does not establish that a particular service can accept this request today. [S7, S1]",
"Consider the surrounding community as well as people who have moved. Hosts may face pressure on shared services, and newcomers may encounter barriers that an apparently equal arrangement does not address. These are questions to investigate, not reasons to assume inevitable conflict or to rank people's worth. Ask whose time, care or travel costs change under the proposal and whether a hidden burden makes participation unrealistic. Do not recommend a loan, business investment or technical livelihood activity without suitable expertise. The practitioner can help clarify priorities, verify a pathway and identify the advice still needed. [S7, R8, S1]",
"Use a short support plan with a person-chosen priority, agreed action, responsible role, capacity check, dependency and review point. Distinguish a verified commitment from a request and record an alternative if the first pathway is unavailable. For example, a document-support enquiry may be feasible while a job placement remains unconfirmed; the plan should show the difference. Avoid counting a sent referral as a restored livelihood. Follow up on whether the person reached the intended support and whether the next step still matches their preference, keeping records proportionate and access controlled. [S7, S1, R4]"],
"Fictional case: a displaced trader wants to restore access to a market and replace a missing document. A directory lists two services, but only the document-support enquiry has a confirmed contact. The plan records the market enquiry as pending and agrees a review instead of promising a job or finance.",
"Create a six-column support plan: priority, action, owner, verified capacity, unresolved condition and review. Include one possible burden on care or travel and a fallback for an unavailable service.",
"The plan should separate a request from an accepted commitment and follow access beyond sending a referral. It should preserve the person's choices without promising a livelihood result or financial product.",["S2","R8","S1","S7","R4"],[
("Livelihoods include care and service access.","A job option conflicts with essential care and inaccessible travel. What should the livelihood discussion examine?","Whether those conditions make the option feasible for the person.",["Only the job title.","An assumption that unpaid care has no practical cost.","A guarantee that taking any job restores a livelihood."],"An option's practical conditions affect whether it can be pursued."),
("A referral needs verified receiving capacity.","An organisation is listed in an old directory. What is needed before describing a referral as accepted?","Confirmation of its current role, capacity and appropriate access process.",["The organisation's name alone.","A promise made by the learner on its behalf.","A larger number of copies of the directory."],"A directory entry does not verify present access."),
("Information sharing should be proportionate and authorised.","A receiving service requests information unrelated to the agreed enquiry. What should be checked?","Necessity and the applicable consent or lawful sharing process before disclosure.",["Automatic disclosure of the entire case record.","Public posting to speed up the enquiry.","An assumption that every referral removes privacy obligations."],"A referral does not justify unrestricted information sharing."),
("Shared services can create different burdens.","A proposed support arrangement adds travel costs for hosts and newcomers. What should the plan do?","Investigate the differing burdens and discuss feasible adjustments.",["Assume inevitable conflict without asking anyone.","Treat all households' costs as identical.","Exclude one group from discussion because of its label."],"Effects need investigation rather than stereotyping."),
("Sending a referral is an output rather than restored livelihood.","A coordinator reports success because an enquiry was sent. What follow-up is most relevant?","Whether the person reached suitable support and the next step still fits their priority.",["Whether the outgoing message had a decorative heading.","Whether sending proves employment was obtained.","Whether the case can be publicised without further review."],"The pathway's actual usefulness requires separate evidence."),
]),
m("Rights participation and durable-solutions review","Review support pathways through informed choice and sustained access rather than movement counts.",[
"Durable-solutions work considers the conditions for people to resume their lives in safety and dignity with access to rights. The Global Protection Cluster guidance discusses return, local integration and settlement elsewhere as pathways requiring informed and voluntary decisions. Their feasibility depends on context and competent assessment. A beginning practitioner should not recommend a pathway solely because it is administratively convenient or publicly described as successful. Explain what is known, what is uncertain, which authority is responsible and how affected people can ask questions or express a different preference without losing appropriate support. [S7, S1]",
"Assessing progress requires more than recording where someone lives. Ask about access to housing, livelihoods, services, documentation and meaningful participation, using the relevant framework and appropriate professional roles. A completed move does not by itself show that a durable solution has been achieved. Physical, legal and material conditions require suitable evidence and expertise; this course does not certify the safety of a destination or resolve a housing dispute. Identify unresolved concerns clearly and use the applicable protection, technical or legal pathway rather than fill a gap with an unsupported assurance. [S7, S2]",
"Participation must allow people to understand alternatives and question the proposed arrangement. Use accessible communication, check understanding and acknowledge constraints on what is currently available. Do not condition essential support on accepting return or relocation, and do not confuse silence with informed agreement. Collective discussions can reveal shared barriers, but sensitive individual concerns need an appropriate channel. The immobility review encourages attention to changing circumstances and aspirations; it does not justify asking people to disclose personal histories in public to demonstrate that their preference is valid. [S7, R4, S1]",
"Agree how the support plan will be reviewed and how concerns can be raised without retaliation. Record the responsible role, a proportionate access or livelihood question, the next review point and what would prompt earlier attention. Respond to urgent concerns through authorised procedures rather than waiting for a routine review. Keep identifiable records restricted and use aggregate reporting carefully where small groups could be recognised. A review may identify progress alongside unresolved barriers. Report both, with the evidence and uncertainty, instead of treating a movement total as a final rights-based judgement. [S7, S1, R8]"],
"Fictional case: a report counts twenty households as a completed relocation. Follow-up finds some cannot reach services or resume earning. The practitioner records these unresolved conditions, checks accessible feedback and refers technical and protection questions rather than certifying a durable solution from the movement count.",
"Write a review note with the person's preferred pathway, two conditions needing evidence, an accountable role, an accessible feedback route and an earlier-review trigger. Explain what the relocation count cannot demonstrate.",
"A defensible review checks sustained rights and access, informed choice and unresolved concerns. It avoids treating a physical move, a signature or a programme label as sufficient evidence of a durable solution.",["S7","S1","S2","R4","R8"],[
("Physical movement does not establish a durable solution.","A programme records twenty completed moves but has no service-access follow-up. Which conclusion is justified?","Movement occurred; sustained rights and access still need evidence.",["Every household has achieved a durable solution.","No further participation is necessary.","A relocation count certifies destination safety."],"Durable solutions concern conditions beyond location."),
("Pathways require informed voluntary decisions.","A worker makes support conditional on accepting the worker's preferred return option. What needs correction?","The coercive condition and the opportunity for an informed choice.",["Only the wording of the publicity notice.","The resident's right to ask about alternatives.","The need to record who issued the condition."],"Assistance should not be used to manufacture agreement."),
("Specialist safety and legal questions need competent assessment.","A destination has unresolved housing and structural concerns. What can a beginning practitioner appropriately do?","Identify the concerns and seek the relevant authorised assessments.",["Certify physical and legal safety from a workshop discussion.","Resolve a housing dispute by counting arrivals.","Replace technical evidence with a general climate citation."],"The support role does not confer technical or legal decision authority."),
("Sensitive feedback needs an appropriate channel.","A resident wants to raise a personal concern outside a public meeting. What should the plan provide?","An accessible confidential route under the applicable protection arrangements.",["A requirement to disclose the concern publicly.","An assumption that silence means informed agreement.","A public list linking residents to complaints."],"Participation includes a safe way to raise sensitive concerns."),
("Review should report mixed findings honestly.","Livelihood access improves while documentation barriers remain. How should the review describe progress?","Report the improvement and unresolved barrier with their evidence and next responsibility.",["Call every issue resolved because one indicator improved.","Remove the barrier from the record.","Declare total failure without examining the improvement."],"A balanced judgement preserves both progress and unfinished work."),
]),
])
